data.content:
These are two separate C# 15 breaking changes:

---

**Error 1 — `left && right` with `dynamic`**

The `&&` operator desugars to `IFilter.operator true(left) ? left : left & right`. Since `right` is `dynamic`, the `&` is deferred to the runtime binder. C# 15 now **rejects** user-defined short-circuiting (`&&`/`||`) when `operator true`/`false` are non-abstract static interface members and an operand is `dynamic` — the dynamic binder cannot discover or invoke non-virtual static members on interfaces, so previous compilers silently produced code that would fail at runtime.

**Fix** — pick one:
```csharp
// Option A: avoid dynamic
void Apply(IFilter left, IFilter right)
{
    var combined = left && right;
}

// Option B: separate the short-circuit manually
void Apply(IFilter left, dynamic right)
{
    if (IFilter.operator true(left)) { /* short-circuit */ }
    else { var combined = left & (IFilter)right; }
}

// Option C: use static abstract/virtual so the runtime binder can resolve them
interface IFilter
{
    static abstract bool operator true(IFilter x);
    static abstract bool operator false(IFilter x);
}
```

---

**Error 2 — `ref readonly` return lambda (CS0518)**

CS0518 means *"predefined type is not defined or imported."* C# 15 changed the metadata encoding for `ref readonly` return lambdas — the compiler now emits a new well-known attribute (in `System.Runtime.CompilerServices`) on the synthesized delegate's return type. If your project references an older `System.Runtime` (e.g., multi-targeting an older TFM, or a stale SDK), that type doesn't exist.

**Fix** — ensure you're actually compiling against the .NET 11 TFM:
```xml
<TargetFramework>net11.0</TargetFramework>
```
and that your SDK is fully updated (`dotnet --version` should show 11.x). If you're multi-targeting older frameworks, guard the lambda:
```csharp
#if NET11_0_OR_GREATER
    var fn = ref readonly int () => ref value;
#else
    // use a named method instead
#endif
```
